  "query_preview": "..\n\nDeep layer subsidence across Lower Mi will persist through Tuesday \nas a broad ridge hold in place. The departure of the upper low over \nthe Mid Atlantic across New England on Tuesday and height falls \nacross the Upper Midwest will actually amplify the mid level ridge \nacross Lower Mi, which will only strengthen the subsidence. Forecast \nmixing depths on model soundings and probabilistic temperature data \nsuggest a continuation of afternoon high temperatures in the low to \nmid 70s Monday and Tuesday, while the large scale subsidence will \nsupport abundant afternoon sunshine. Late night and morning fog will \nbe the primary forecast concerns the next few mornings. NNE flow off \nLake Huron will persist through tonight. Given the response off the \nlake this morning and considering dewpoints across the region are a \nfew degrees higher than yesterday, another stratus/fog plume off \nLake Huron and Saginaw Bay is forecast to develop tonight/Monday \nmorning. A little stronger winds atop the nocturnal inversion and \nslight backing of the winds toward the north may advect the \nstratus/fog layer farther south into Metro Detroit. The low level \nwind fields will be much weaker Mon night/Tuesday morning as high \npressure settles overhead, reducing the risk of advective fog. \nHowever, residual moisture in the nocturnal boundary layer capped by \nideal radiative cooling potential will raise the risk of pure \nradiation fog development. \n\nPhasing of a southern stream long wave trough with the remnants of \nHurricane Polo will transport a tropical moisture plume into the \nsouthern and central high plains Tuesday and will increase moisture \nadvection into a low to mid level frontal boundary forecast to \nprogress across the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes region in the \nThursday to Friday time frame. The front will be driven across the \nregion in response to the northern stream wave tracking across nrn \nOntario. Differences in amplitude and timing of this wave leads to \nlarge spread among the model ensembles in timing the frontal system \nacross Lower Mi. Trends in the latest probabilistic guidance are \nsupporting a later timing, leading to higher chances of an all out \ndry and warm day Wednesday. The potential for good frontal dynamics \nwithin the high theta e axis along the front will support a good \nchance for rain. Within the latest probability space, that is \nexpected sometime in the Thursday to Friday time frame. High spread \nin ensemble 24 hour forecast QFP values (ranging anywhere from a \nquarter inch to 2 inches) is a result of the variation in ensemble \nmembers as to how progressive this front will be across the southern \nlakes.",
